Subject: A scissor of no area lets nothing pass

MTLScissorRect with a width or a height of 0 draws nothing. Through the C
interface a rectangle of no width was the core's "width 0: the whole target"
-- which is what no rectangle at all is -- so the draw covered the target; one
of no height was refused, a draw left out with a line in the console.

Draw::scissorEmpty says what such a rectangle is (the C interface sets it for
either side 0; in the core a width with no height says the same). The draw is
written like any other, with the screen scissor turnip writes for an empty one,
the top left past the bottom right: (1, 1)-(0, 0) (tu_pipeline.cc:2669-2672).
It opens its pass, Clear and all, touches no pixel (render::drawArea), and in a
binned pass no tile's visibility stream lets it through. The fake takes that
scissor for what it is, and a draw whose scissors leave it no pixel reaches no
tile there.

Two tests: the registers, a command buffer in sysmem and a binned pass of tiles
(the tiles run the other draws and skip this one, each of them); and the C
interface's rectangles of no width and of no height among others. The refusal's
old expectation goes; the random frames, which ask for such scissors among
their tweaks, now draw 230 draws more and refuse 230 fewer, the same from kept
states as written whole. No golden stream of a scene changes.


